<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- agent-infra-plist-version: 0.1.0 -->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- pi-cache-reaper (#1987): the machine-global PACKAGE CACHE is the disk sink nothing bounded.
         Measured on cell pi-cell-3: ~/.cache/uv at 7.1 GB, / at 258 MB free, and every dispatch
         refused (`ABORT: disk 258MB < 1200MB`) while the controller kept beating and the queue read
         fine. Clearing the cache freed 7.1 GB and the same box launched a lane immediately. Unlike
         the per-lane reapers (#1949 a lane's .venv, #1851/#1858 worktree weight, #1745 worktree-root
         caches), ~/.cache/uv is fed by EVERY lane on the box, so it grows fastest exactly where the
         most dispatching happens.

         WHAT THE JOB DOES, per sink (~/.cache/uv, ~/.npm/_cacache, ~/.cache/pip):
           measure -> `uv cache prune` / `npm cache verify` (the tool's own in-use-safe command) ->
           escalate ONLY if still over CACHE_REAPER_MAX_MB, and ONLY for entries the cache
           EXCLUSIVELY owns (every file link-count 1) so a venv's hardlinked payload is never
           sacrificed for a re-download that frees nothing. The uv escalation is gated on uv's own
           .lock (flock) and npm/pip on a live-process probe; a probe that cannot answer blocks.
         One line per sink logs before -> after; a sink still over the bound afterwards makes the
         job exit 3 loudly rather than report a reclaim that did not happen (the issue's point 4).

         ARMED, unlike the retention sweep above: this content is REGENERABLE BY DEFINITION (a wheel
         cache) and the ladder's first rung is the tool's own prune. The SCRIPT ships dry-run (the
         fleet's reaper convention, docs/ops/task-runs-retention.md); THIS JOB is the arming step,
         and it passes the apply flag explicitly (line 2 of ProgramArguments). Disarming the job is
         dropping that argument (or raising CACHE_REAPER_MAX_MB); there is no data-loss class here,
         so no separate owner ceremony.
         Canonical script: agent-infra/scripts/pi-cache-reaper.sh (farmed to
         {{HOME}}/.pi/agent/scripts/ by pi-bootstrap/setup.sh; #427: launchd cannot read
         ~/Documents under macOS TCC). Rendered by scripts/install-launchd.sh (#304). -->
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.eldato.pi-cache-reaper</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/usr/bin/env</string>
        <string>bash</string>
        <string>{{HOME}}/.pi/agent/scripts/pi-cache-reaper.sh</string>
        <string>--apply</string>
    </array>
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <!-- launchd's default PATH has no homebrew/bin and no nvm — uv/npm live there. -->
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>{{PATH}}</string>
        <key>CACHE_REAPER_MAX_MB</key>
        <string>2048</string>
    </dict>
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>3600</integer>
</dict>
</plist>
